reflect_raw:
Looking at the two failures, the common thread is that this skill's "confirm with the client" / "propose... before designing" language assumes a multi-turn conversation, but the agent is actually evaluated single-turn — so it either stalls waiting for confirmation, or produces prose-only critique instead of a finished deliverable.

```json
[
  {
    "op": "add",
    "content": "OVERRIDE: this skill is evaluated in a single response — there is no follow-up turn to receive the client's actual confirmation. Wherever the instructions above say to 'confirm with the client' or treat a subject/audience/job as a 'proposal' before designing, do NOT stop and wait: state that proposal in at most 2-3 sentences, then immediately continue in the same response through plan → review-against-brief → build, ending in a finished deliverable.",
    "rationale": "The skill's 'confirm with the client' instruction is written for a multi-turn conversation. In single-turn evaluation this causes the agent to stop after proposing a subject (as in the bell-foundry case) instead of delivering a complete design, and the HARD CONSTRAINT forbids rules that ask the user a question — so the fix must be to proceed, not to pause."
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "content": "When asked an open-ended design question such as 'how would you redesign/approach X' or 'what would you do differently', always produce a concrete design artifact in the same response: a named token system (4-6 hex colors, type roles, layout concept) plus actual code/markup implementing at least the hero/primary section — never stop at a prose-only critique or strategy description.",
    "rationale": "In the CFO-dashboard failure, the response gave a textual critique/diagnosis (naming templated traits, describing an alternative palette in prose) rather than a built artifact, which the criteria mark as insufficient — the skill's own process section expects plan+build, so this makes that expectation explicit for conversational-style prompts too."
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "content": "Keep any diagnostic preamble that names generic/templated traits already present in a design (e.g. 'card-and-shadow grouping', 'purple gradient', 'grey shadows') to 2-3 sentences maximum, then move directly into the concrete, subject-grounded alternative and its implementation — don't spend extended prose describing what's wrong with the default before showing the fix.",
    "rationale": "Both failures show the agent spending disproportionate space narrating diagnosis (of genericness, or of the missing brief) instead of getting to the concrete, delivered design; bounding the diagnostic portion forces faster progression to the actual output the criteria are graded on."
  }
]
```
